  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 124 PM CST Tue Nov 24 2026\n\nOn Thursday night the growing 500 MB low will drift east\nnortheastward, then swing up into New England Friday and Friday\nnight. In its wake, brief ridging will move over Iowa bringing a\nquiet period from Thursday night through Friday. This should\nresult in clearing skies and moderating temperatures on Friday,\nwith highs in the mid to upper 40s being about 5-10 degrees \nwarmer than on the previous days. From Friday night through the\nweekend, a broad 500 MB trough will then carve out across\ncentral Canada and the northern U.S., with at least three\ndistinct shortwave impulses rounding the base of the trough over\nIowa. This will result in fairly steady temperatures, perhaps a\nbit warmer on Saturday, along with intermittent precipitation\nchances through the weekend. Details on these chances remain\nhighly variable at this range, with various model runs depicting\nlittle to no QPF in Iowa while others sometimes paint several\ntenths of an inch at various times, with a large spread in\nforecast temperatures also leading to uncertainty in what type\nthat precipitation would fall as. For now, the official forecast\nretains broad PoPs roughly around Saturday night when the second\nand apparently strongest wave will move overhead, and also\npredominantly rain as the p-type, however, that will depend\nheavily on storm track and temperatures as solutions converge in\nthe coming days.",
